  "IR_Temp_Vuln" [label="(0,0,0) If resistance-based, then vulnerable to temp."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes and evaluates a ratiometric gas-sensing strategy based on the organic–inorganic hybrid covalent superlattice AgBDT, whose defect-engineered NO2 sensitivity and strong photoelectric response allow gas-induced resistance change to be internally normalized by light-induced conductance, aiming for ppb-level detection while compensating temperature-driven baseline drift.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Temperature drift creates a need for internal normalization, and AgBDT's coupled gas-sensing and photoelectric functions provide the material basis for a ratiometric sensor design."];
  "Node_AgBDT_Dual" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: Given enhanced photo and gas sensitivity, therefore AgBDT delivers dual responses."];
  "Node_AgBDT_Gas" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: Given AgBDT and rule, therefore AgBDT has gas sensitivity."];
  "Node_AgBDT_Photo" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: Given AgBDT and rule, therefore AgBDT has enhanced photo."];
  "Node_Ratio_Benefit" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: Given ratio technique and rule, therefore Ratiometry reduces errors."];
  "Node_Super_Photo" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: Given superlattice and rule, therefore Superlattice has photo capability."];
  "Node_Temp_Vulnerable" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: Given resistance-based and rule, therefore Chemiresistive vulnerable to temp."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Gas sensing's dependence on defects and light sensing's preference for minimized defects create a defect-design tension."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) A defect-design tension between gas and light sensing makes synthesis challenging, rather than impossible."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) AgBDT has functional-group-enabled gas-sensing capability through sulfhydryl groups."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) The superlattice platform is motivated as a way to balance photoelectric response with gas-sensing functionality."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) AgBDT's dual photoelectric and gas responses support formulating a ratiometric gas-sensing approach that uses photoelectric response as an internal reference."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) A ratiometric AgBDT gas-sensing approach with a photoelectric reference is expected to mitigate temperature-fluctuation interference."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Temperature vulnerability in resistance-based chemiresistive sensing provides a plausible route by which drift can compromise sensing precision."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) The repaired reasoning supports proposing an AgBDT ratiometric gas sensor in which photoelectric response serves as a reference to reduce temperature interference."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Temperature-compensation approaches that require extra circuitry can add cost, although compensation may also involve other burdens such as complex algorithms."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Conventional temperature compensation can mitigate drift-related issues, but its need for added circuitry, cost, or algorithmic complexity makes it a limited practical remedy."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Because conventional temperature compensation is practically limited, achieving self-tuning temperature compensation within a single sensing material remains a challenge."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Because ratiometry reduces errors from varying conditions, it can help overcome accuracy loss in sensing measurements."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) The challenge of single-material self-tuning motivates using a reference factor that can neutralize drift in a ratiometric gas sensor."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) A material with enhanced photoelectric response, such as AgBDT, is a plausible source of the reference signal needed for ratiometric drift neutralization."];
